\DOC BOUNDER_TAC

\TYPE {BOUNDER_TAC : thm list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{BOUNDER_TAC} closes an arithmetic goal asserting numeric bounds by automatic
interval reasoning.

\DESCRIBE
The single argument has type {thm list}: theorems supplied alongside the goal,
in the observed successful traces either empty or a collection of facts drawn
from the surrounding context; the goal's own assumptions are left untouched.
The goal's conclusion {t} must be a bound assertion — a single {<=}/{<}/{>}
comparison, or a conjunction of such comparisons, between arithmetic
expressions over naturals, integers and reals built from word/bit operations,
sums, products, divisions and literals.
{
           A ?- t
      ==================  BOUNDER_TAC thl
}
\noindent It never splits the goal or rewrites it in place; every successful
trace closes it outright, with assumption lists ranging from none to large
machine-state contexts that play no visible role.

\FAILURE
Fails when the conclusion is not of the bound form it can handle — for instance
an equation between non-arithmetic terms — where the internal normalisation of
the conclusion raises an error; and when the conclusion is a bound assertion
whose truth it cannot establish from the available information, where it
reports that no tactic applied. Both failures arise from the tactic itself
rather than from the supplied theorem list, which in the failing traces
includes both an empty list and a long list of context facts.

\ENDDOC
